{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3546","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3546","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3546\",\"parent_revision\":\"36e37064c6b9d0a531ba978dc48be1560c0f1c7825ffabcabb3fde42ea693564\",\"root_revision\":\"36e37064c6b9d0a531ba978dc48be1560c0f1c7825ffabcabb3fde42ea693564\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nGitHub GraphQL is blocked in Claude Code agent sessions. Every `dh` code path that reaches\\nGraphQL fails there, and the failure is currently indistinguishable from a generic GraphQL error.\\n\\nAdd detection for this specific condition, then fall back to REST under two rules:\\n\\n1. **A GraphQL call whose data one REST call can also get or set** \u2014 swap the function to REST\\n   outright. GraphQL buys nothing there.\\n2. **A GraphQL call that replaces multiple REST calls** \u2014 keep GraphQL first, and add an\\n   efficient asynchronous REST fallback that runs only when detection fires.\\n\\nGraphQL exists in this codebase because it cuts request count. That reason stands, so rule 2\\npreserves it rather than discarding GraphQL everywhere.\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**Systems Inventory**\\n\\n| System | Path | Change |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Chokepoint | `backlog_core/gh_client.py:490` `_graphql_request` | Detect the blocked-GraphQL 403 and raise a distinct error type |\\n| Protocol surface | `backlog_core/backend_types.py:266-330` `GitHubExtras` | 13 `*_graphql` members to classify and route |\\n| Operations facade | `backlog_core/operations.py:164-380` | Re-exports each `*_graphql` member |\\n| SAM task backend | `sam_schema/core/backends/github_task.py:255,382,430,465,474,583` | 6 call sites |\\n| dh operations | `dh_core/operations.py:1427,1430,1541` | 3 `sync_issues_graphql` call sites |\\n| Error classification | `backlog_core/sync_state.py:265,365` | Already reasons about `_graphql_request` failures |\\n\\n**Not in scope:** `backlog_core/linear_client.py` targets Linear's GraphQL API, not GitHub's, and\\nthe proxy block does not apply to it.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n1. A named error type distinguishes \\\"GraphQL is unavailable in this environment\\\" from every other\\n   GraphQL failure.\\n2. Detection reads the HTTP status and the response message, not a flattened string.\\n3. Detection lives at the `_graphql_request` chokepoint, so no call site repeats it.\\n4. Every Rule 1 function issues a REST call and no GraphQL call.\\n5. Every Rule 2 function attempts GraphQL first and falls back to REST only on the detected error.\\n6. Rule 2 fallbacks issue their REST calls concurrently, not serially.\\n7. The node-ID-to-number question is resolved and documented.\\n8. A test proves the detector matches the real 403 signature and rejects a generic 403.\\n9. A test proves each Rule 2 fallback produces the same result shape as its GraphQL path.\\n10. `uv run pytest`, `ruff check`, `ruff format --check`, and `ty check` all pass.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Reproducibility\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n```bash\\ngh api graphql -f query='{viewer{login}}'\\ncurl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' -X POST https://api.github.com/graphql -d '{\\\"query\\\":\\\"{viewer{login}}\\\"}'\\n```\\n\\nBoth return HTTP 403. The response body carries a fixed message:\\n\\n```text\\nGitHub GraphQL is not available from Claude Code sessions; use the REST API\\n(gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/...). ...\\ndocumentation_url: https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code/github-actions\\n```\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session investigation, 2026-09-14\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Related**: #519 (shared GitHub client) fixes TLS for REST only; this issue covers the\\n  GraphQL half of the same environment problem.\\n\\n\\n---\\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)_\\n</div>\\n\\n## Root-Cause Analysis\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`backlog_core/gh_client.py:490` holds `_graphql_request`, the single chokepoint every GraphQL\\npath goes through. On line 513 it catches `GithubException` and re-raises\\n`BacklogError(f\\\"GraphQL request failed: {exc}\\\")`.\\n\\nThat wrapper discards the HTTP status and flattens the distinguishing message into free text, so\\nno caller can tell \\\"GraphQL is blocked in this environment, retry over REST\\\" apart from\\n\\\"this query is malformed\\\" or \\\"the network is down\\\". Detection has to happen at the\\n`GithubException` boundary, where `exc.status` is still 403 and the message is still intact.\\n\\nThe file already carries `_is_not_found_error(error: BacklogError)` at line 527, which inspects a\\nwrapped message for a 404-equivalent condition. A sibling detector fits that established shape,\\nbut should read the status rather than the flattened string.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Proposed Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nBucket assignments below are derived from the `GitHubExtras` Protocol signatures. Each one needs\\nconfirmation against its implementation before the swap.\\n\\n**Rule 1 \u2014 swap to REST outright (one GraphQL call, one REST call):**\\n\\n| Function | REST equivalent |\\n|---|---|\\n| `_fetch_issue_graphql` | `GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/{number}` |\\n| `_update_issue_graphql` | `PATCH /repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/{number}` |\\n| `_add_comment_graphql` | `POST /repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/{number}/comments` |\\n| `_fetch_comment_by_id_graphql` | `GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/comments/{id}` |\\n| `_update_issue_comment_graphql` | `PATCH /repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/comments/{id}` |\\n| `_fetch_milestones_graphql` | `GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/milestones` |\\n\\n**Rule 2 \u2014 keep GraphQL first, add async REST fallback (one GraphQL call, many REST calls):**\\n\\n| Function | Why REST costs more |\\n|---|---|\\n| `sync_issues_graphql` | Bulk issue sync; REST needs paginated list plus per-issue detail |\\n| `_update_issues_graphql_batch` | One mutation per update over REST |\\n| `_fetch_issues_graphql` | Paginated list, and nested fields need extra calls |\\n| `_fetch_issue_comments_graphql` | Paginated, and interleaves with issue fetch |\\n| `_resolve_labels_graphql` | Resolves many labels in one round trip |\\n| `_fetch_blobs_graphql` | Batch blob fetch; REST is one call per blob |\\n</div>\\n\\n## Open Design Question\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nGraphQL mutations address objects by **node ID**; REST addresses them by **number**. Six of the\\nfunctions above take a node ID (`issue_node_id`, `comment_node_id`, `node[\\\"id\\\"]`). A REST fallback\\nneeds the number, so the design must settle one of:\\n\\n- carry the number alongside the node ID through the call chain, or\\n- decode the number from the node ID, or\\n- spend one extra REST lookup to translate.\\n\\nThis is unresolved and needs a decision before the Rule 1 swaps land.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"1383fc63f7a63ad558e968966057462ba94957eb0fa94e22f1e61d6b144b911b\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABVB3Hiw\"}"}